### Presence Is a Different Scaling Problem Than Document Content — It's High-Frequency and Disposable

The key property that makes presence data tractable at scale: unlike document edits, a stale cursor position is worthless the instant a newer one arrives — there's no history to preserve, no conflict to resolve, just "what's true right now". That means presence can use fire-and-forget, latest-value-wins semantics instead of the durable, ordered delivery a document CRDT needs — a much cheaper guarantee to provide at scale.

The fan-out bottleneck is architectural: a single server process holding all WebSocket connections for a popular document caps out — you need a pub-sub layer (Redis Pub/Sub, or a dedicated message broker) so any server instance handling a client's connection can publish a cursor update once, and every other instance subscribed to that document's channel relays it to its own connected clients, decoupling "how many users are viewing this document" from "how many connections one process can hold".

```typescript
// Any server instance can handle any client; Redis fans out across instances
io.on('connection', (socket) => {
  socket.on('cursor:move', ({ docId, x, y }) => {
    redisPub.publish(`doc:${docId}:presence`, JSON.stringify({ userId: socket.userId, x, y }));
  });
});

// Each instance subscribes once per document it has active viewers for
redisSub.subscribe(`doc:${docId}:presence`, (message) => {
  io.to(`doc:${docId}`).emit('cursor:update', JSON.parse(message)); // relay to this instance's local clients
});
```

Bandwidth per client is the second axis, and it's solved differently: throttle cursor broadcasts to a fixed rate (e.g. 20/second) regardless of how fast the source cursor is actually moving, and only broadcast to clients currently viewing that specific document/viewport, not every connected user globally. A document with 500 concurrent viewers all broadcasting cursor moves at full mouse-event frequency (60+/second) would be ~30,000 messages/second fanned out to 500 clients each — throttling at the source cuts that by an order of magnitude before it ever reaches the fan-out layer.

```typescript
const throttledBroadcast = throttle((docId: string, cursorData: CursorData) => {
  redisPub.publish(`doc:${docId}:presence`, JSON.stringify(cursorData));
}, 50); // max 20 broadcasts/sec per user, regardless of raw mousemove frequency
```

**Common pitfall:** Broadcasting every raw `mousemove` event without client-side throttling — mouse events fire far faster than any UI needs to visually update (60+/second), so an unthrottled broadcast multiplies message volume by an order of magnitude for no perceptible benefit, and is usually what actually causes the pub-sub layer or client bandwidth to become the bottleneck, not the number of concurrent users itself.

