### CSP Limits *Where* Code Can Come From; SRI Verifies *What* Arrived Hasn't Changed — Neither Alone Is Enough

A supply-chain attack on a third-party script (analytics SDK, payment widget, ad tag) is dangerous specifically because the browser trusts it with the same privileges as your own first-party code — it can read cookies, intercept form input, exfiltrate data, all without your own code doing anything wrong. The two complementary defenses target different parts of the problem: CSP restricts which origins are even allowed to load a script at all; SRI verifies that the script actually served from an allowed origin matches an expected hash, so if the CDN hosting it gets compromised and starts serving different bytes, the browser refuses to execute it.

```typescript
// CSP header: restrict script sources to an explicit allowlist
// Content-Security-Policy: script-src 'self' https://cdn.trusted-analytics.com;

// SRI: verify the exact bytes match what you audited, not just the origin
<script
  src="https://cdn.trusted-analytics.com/sdk.js"
  integrity="sha384-oqVuAfXRKap7fdgcCY5uykM6+R9GqQ8K/uxy9rx7HNQlGYl1kPzQho1wx4JwY8wC"
  crossorigin="anonymous"
></script>
```

The gap SRI doesn't close: it verifies the script hash matches at *load time*, but a script's behavior isn't just what's in that file — if the compromised code lives in something the SDK fetches or `eval`s dynamically at runtime (rather than in the initially-loaded file), SRI never sees it, because SRI only pins the integrity of the resource named in the `<script>` tag itself, not of anything that script subsequently loads. That's exactly why a strict CSP without `unsafe-eval` and with a locked-down `connect-src` matters as a second layer — it limits what a compromised (but SRI-passing) script can actually exfiltrate to, or dynamically load from, even if its initial bytes were legitimate.

The gap CSP doesn't close on its own: it's origin-based, so if the compromised script is served from a domain you've already allowlisted (the SDK vendor's own CDN, compromised at their end rather than via a CDN substitution), CSP does nothing — the origin is legitimately allowed, the code inside it just isn't what you think it is. That's the case SRI specifically covers.

```typescript
// CSP restricts exfiltration surface even for an SRI-passing but logically compromised script
// Content-Security-Policy: connect-src 'self' https://api.trusted-analytics.com; script-src 'self' https://cdn.trusted-analytics.com 'strict-dynamic';
```

A practical mitigation neither CSP nor SRI covers: pinning the third-party script to a specific, audited version rather than a `latest`/unversioned CDN URL, so a vendor's own compromise doesn't automatically propagate to you the next time their CDN serves an updated file — you control the timing of picking up new versions, after re-auditing.

**Common pitfall:** Adding SRI to a script tag but not pinning it to an exact version, or vice versa — SRI on an unversioned/`@latest` URL is actively counterproductive: the hash you computed will simply mismatch and break the page the moment the vendor legitimately updates the file, so teams often end up removing SRI rather than pinning the version, silently losing the protection.

